.Im MicroEmacs command|transposition
.so me.tni
.TH generate-hash 2
.XI generate-hash - "Generate SHA1 or SHA256 hash or HMAC"
.SH NAME
generate-hash \- Generate SHA1 or SHA256 hash or HMAC
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.I n
.B generate-hash
[ "\fIkey\fR" ] [ "\fIdata\fR" ]
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B generate-hash
computes  the SHA256 hash value of the current  buffer's  content. The hash is then turned into a hex string and the
.Ht $result 5
variable is set to the resultant 64 byte string.
.PP
The hash of a text buffer is of its text rather than the file's bytes, so the same text gives the same hash on every
platform. The text is hashed in UTF-8 and every line end as a single \fC\\n\fR (0x0a) character, whatever the buffer's
encoding and its
.Ht cr 2m ,
.Ht lf 2m
and
.Ht ctrlz 2m
modes. A line with no line end, the last line of a file with no final newline or a long line split when the file
was loaded, adds no \fC\\n\fR. When the hash of a file's exact bytes is required, load the file with
.Ht rbin 2m
or
.Ht binary 2m
mode enabled, the hash of such a buffer is the hash of the bytes it holds.
.PP
The numeric  argument  \fIn\fR is a bit based flag used to modify the default  behaviour of the  command,  where the bits are defined as
follows:
.IP \fB0x01\fR
If bit 2 is clear this defines  whether the hash will be generated  for the current  region (if clear) or whole buffer
(if set). If bit 2 is set then this bit  determines  whether the input  \fIdata\fR  is a hex-string  (if set) or plain
text.
.IP \fB0x02\fR
When set the user is prompted  for the  \fIdata\fR,  in plain text or  hex-string  form (see bit 0x01) rather than the
current region or buffer.
.IP \fB0x04\fR
When set a HMAC is calculated instead, the user will be prompted for the \fIkey\fR.
.IP \fB0x08\fR
When calculating a HMAC, setting this bit indicates the \fIkey\fR is given in the form of a hex-string.
.IP \fB0x10\fR
When set a SHA1 based hash/HMAC is calculated instead.
.SH EXAMPLE
To calculate the hash of a string use:
.RS
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
2 generate-hash "Hello world"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.RE
.PP
After executing the SHA256 hash value will be stored in \fB$result\fR. To calculate a HMAC-SHA256 of a string use:
.RS
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
6 generate-hash "TheKey" "Hello world"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.RE
.PP
After executing \fB$result\fR will be set to \fC1804C1869D6DD76A9A8D00819E6F62E970D981F01FE805F927CF4E3BDF4AD95C\fR.
.SH NOTES
The \fIkey\fR and \fIdata\fR strings are hashed as their bytes, a character above 0x7f in its UTF-8 form, and a byte
given by
.Ht &hexb 4 ,
including 0, as that byte. Alternatively set bit 0x01 or 0x08 and give the \fIdata\fR or \fIkey\fR as a hex-string.
.PP
An 8-bit build of \*(mS holds a file's bytes as its text, so a file written in a code page other than ASCII or
UTF-8 hashes differently to the Unicode build; ASCII and UTF-8 files give the same hash in both. An 8-bit build
cannot hold a 0 byte in a text buffer.
.PP
When calculating the hash of a region, if
.Ht binary 2m
or
.Ht rbin 2m
modes are enabled then the whole of the region's  first line is always  included , as is the whole of the last line if
the region's end point column is greater than 0. If neither of these modes are enabled then each line end within the
region is hashed as a \fC\\n\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht $result 5 ,
.Ht &hexb 4 ,
.Ht binary 2m ,
.Ht rbin 2m ,
.Ht cr 2m ,
.Ht lf 2m ,
.Ht ctrlz 2m ,
.Ht buffer-mode 2 ,
.Hr SHA1 3 ,
.Hr SHA256 3 ,
.Hr HMAC-SHA256 3 .
.ad
.FH
